=begin pod

=TITLE App::ModuleAudit Usage

=head1 DATABASE

By default, C<module-audit> uses:

=begin code
~/.module-audit/module-audit.db
=end code

Override the database path with C<--db-path>.

=head1 SCANNING

=begin code :lang<bash>
module-audit scan
=end code

=head1 LISTING

=begin code :lang<bash>
module-audit list
module-audit list --repeat-headings
=end code

The list command shows installed modules and prints a summary line.

=head1 UPGRADE CHECKING

=begin code :lang<bash>
module-audit check-upgrades
module-audit report
=end code

=head1 PRUNING OLD VERSIONS

=begin code :lang<bash>
module-audit prune-versions --name=JSON::Fast
module-audit prune-versions --all
module-audit prune-versions --all --dry-run=False --force
module-audit prune-versions --all --interactive --dry-run=False --force
=end code

=head1 APPLYING A MODULE FILE

A module file may contain simple names or full C<zef>-style identities:

=begin code
JSON::Fast
Cro::HTTP
Some::Module:ver<1.2.3>:auth<zef:AUTHOR>
=end code

Blank lines and lines beginning with C<#> are ignored.

=begin code :lang<bash>
module-audit apply-file --file=modules.txt
module-audit apply-file --file=modules.txt --dry-run=False --force
module-audit apply-file --file=modules.txt --interactive --dry-run=False --force
=end code

=head1 SAFETY

Commands that modify installed modules are dry-run by default or require
C<--force> for actual changes.



=head2 Prune verification

After each uninstall attempt, C<prune-versions> verifies the requested older
version against fresh C<zef list --installed> output. The summary separates
successful removals from already-absent targets, failed C<zef> commands,
not-verified removals, and skipped targets.


=head1 INTEGRATION TESTING

The file C<t/04-prune-behavior.t> creates a temporary local module,
installs multiple versions with C<zef>, scans them, and exercises
C<prune-versions>. The final uninstall verification is marked TODO until
the exact local C<zef> version-removal behavior is settled.


=end pod
